"A dimensional schema diagram organizes analytic facts around dimensions while stating the grain of each fact table.\n\nUse when: Design a warehouse model for consistent business analysis. Review join paths and measure aggregation behavior.\n\nAvoid when: The main purpose is normalized transactional updates.\n\nVisual grammar: A central fact table names its grain and measures. Dimension tables carry descriptive attributes and surrogate keys where used. Relationships show join cardinality, including snowflaked dimension paths when intentional.\n\nBuild: Write the fact grain in one explicit sentence. Select dimensions that describe each fact at that grain. Add measures and identify nonadditive or semiadditive behavior. Place dimensions around facts with separate join corridors and clear key labels.\n\nCheck: Every fact row conforms to the declared grain. Joins do not silently multiply measures. Historical dimension treatment is stated where relevant. Relationship endpoints and measure text are never crossed by connectors.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing order-level and line-level measures in one unexplained grain. Treating all numeric columns as safely additive facts.\n\nExample: SalesLine facts at one sold item per transaction join Date, Store, Customer, and Product dimensions.\n\nAlternatives: Relational schema diagram, Data vault diagram."
